---
import { AUTHOR } from '../site.config.js';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About & methodology — The Stat Method"
  description="Why this site exists, how it is funded, and how every calculator is built.">
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="article-grid">
      <article class="article">
        <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
        <h1>Why this site exists.</h1>

        <!-- Mission stated as commitments rather than outcomes. A health site cannot
             honestly promise to improve anyone's health; it can promise how it will
             behave. Every line below is checkable against the site itself, which is
             the point — a mission nobody can hold you to is decoration. -->
        <aside class="mission">
          <span class="eyebrow">Our mission</span>
          <p class="mission-lede">
            To make the arithmetic behind health, fitness and nutrition advice
            visible, checkable, and free — so you can judge a number instead of
            trusting it.
          </p>
          <p class="mission-body">
            We will always name the equation behind a result, cite the paper it came
            from, say what it cannot tell you, and keep the tools free and free of
            signups. When the evidence is weak or disputed, we will say so rather than
            round it into confidence. When we get something wrong, we will correct it
            in public.
          </p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Most macro calculators hand you three numbers and no reasoning. Ask where the
            protein figure came from and you get a shrug, or a checkout page.</p>
          <p>The Stat Method is the opposite trade: every tool shows its equation, every article
            cites its sources, and nothing is behind a signup. The calculators are open
            source — you can read exactly what they do before you trust what they say.</p>

          <h2>The math is free forever</h2>
          <p>Calculations run in your browser and cost us nothing per user, so they will
            never be paywalled. If we ever charge for anything, it will be for things that
            genuinely cost money to run — saving your history, syncing across devices,
            exporting your data. Never for a formula.</p>

          <h2>How it's funded</h2>
          <p>Ads below the fold on articles, a donation link, and occasional affiliate links
            on gear worth owning. Never more than two ad units on a page, never beside a
            results panel, and never on a calculator page. If a recommendation is paid,
            it says so inline.</p>

          <h2>What this isn't</h2>
          <p>Medical advice. Equations are fitted to population averages, so treat any output
            as a starting point and adjust from two to three weeks of your own data. If you
            have a medical condition, talk to a clinician who knows your history.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <aside class="aside">
        <div class="box">
          <h3>Open source</h3>
          <a href={`https://github.com/${AUTHOR.handle}`} rel="noopener">Every calculator on GitHub →</a>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
